    id: "want",
    title: "Want and need: 欲, 愛, 想欲",
    blurb: "Say what you want to do, what you like, what you would like and what you don't want.",
    deck: "Night market food",
    body: `
<p>English has "want", "would like", "like", "need to" and "be going to". Taigi covers them with three words, and each one goes right before the verb. None of them changes for the person or for time: 我欲, 你欲 and 伊欲 are all the same word. The table sums them up; the sections below show each one at work.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Taigi</th><th>Tâi-lô</th><th>Main English meanings</th></tr><tr><td class="k">欲</td><td>beh</td><td>want to; going to; about to</td></tr><tr><td class="k">想欲</td><td>siūnn-beh</td><td>would like to</td></tr><tr><td class="k">愛</td><td>ài</td><td>like to, love to; must</td></tr><tr><td class="k">無愛</td><td>bô ài</td><td>don't want; don't like</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>欲: want to, going to</h3>
<p><b>欲</b> <i>beh</i> + verb is "want to" or "going to". It is the word for ordering and for plans. With the weather, it means "about to": 矣 at the end shows the change is coming.</p>
{{ex:我欲食炒飯，你咧？|Guá beh tsia̍h tshá-pn̄g, lí--leh?|I'll have fried rice, and you?}}
{{ex:我欲啉茶。|Guá beh lim tê.|I want to drink tea.}}
{{ex:欲落雨矣。|Beh lo̍h-hōo--ah.|It's about to rain.}}
<h3>想欲: would like</h3>
<p><b>想欲</b> <i>siūnn-beh</i> is softer, like "I'd like to". Use it for polite wishes.</p>
{{ex:我想欲去便所。|Guá siūnn-beh khì piān-sóo.|I'd like to go to the restroom.}}
<h3>愛: like, love, and must</h3>
<p><b>愛</b> <i>ài</i> + verb means "like to" or "love to". In advice it means "must, need to": 你行路愛小心 is "you must be careful when you walk".</p>
{{ex:我真愛𨑨迌。|Guá tsin ài tshit-thô.|I really love having fun.}}
{{ex:你行路愛小心。|Lí kiânn-lōo ài sió-sim.|Be careful when you walk.}}
<h3>無愛: don't want</h3>
<p>English uses "don't" for every "not want". In Taigi, the negative of 愛 is <b>無愛</b> <i>bô ài</i>, "don't want, don't like". 毋 means refusing (won't), which sounds much stronger.</p>
{{ex:我無愛食肉。|Guá bô ài tsia̍h bah.|I don't want to eat meat.}}
<h3>Yes/no questions</h3>
<p>Add 無 at the end, as in lesson 14: <b>你欲去無？</b> <i>Lí beh khì--bô?</i> "Do you want to go?". The 無 is said light, in the neutral tone. The same works for 愛: ask with 無 at the end, and answer 愛 or 無愛.</p>
<h3>Ordering at the night market</h3>
<p>To order, say 欲 + verb + number + classifier + food. The classifier is the container: a bowl is 碗, a cup is 杯.</p>
{{ex:我欲食兩碗麵。|Guá beh tsia̍h nn̄g uánn mī.|I'll have two bowls of noodles.}}
{{ex:我欲啉一杯茶。|Guá beh lim tsi̍t pue tê.|I'll have a cup of tea.}}
<p>If you know some Mandarin, you will reach for 要. In Taigi, write and say 欲. The same goes for the verbs: eat is 食 and drink is 啉.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using the Mandarin word. <b>我要食炒飯</b> → <b>我欲食炒飯</b>.</div>
`,
